\section{A Word Enters the Machine}

Take a single word: \emph{mother}.

Chapter~1 already argued that in these systems meaning is not a ghost behind the sign, but a location in a shared space. The sign has an address. What it did not yet show is what happens to that address when language is used.

Inside a contemporary transformer, \emph{mother} does not sit in a static catalogue. It moves.

At the outer edge of the system, the visible string is broken into one or more tokens from a fixed vocabulary. Each of those tokens is associated with a learned vector in a space with thousands of dimensions. These vectors were carved out during pre-training and already encode which other symbols have tended to surround this token, in what syntactic frames, and in which institutional registers.

At the moment the model is called, \emph{mother} thus begins as one point in a vast learned terrain. It is the same initial point whether we write:

\begin{quote}
\emph{My mother called.}

\emph{The mother cell divided.}

\emph{Mother, may I?}
\end{quote}

What differs is who stands around it.

The input sequence---system instructions, retrieved documents, previous turns, current user text---is mapped into a cloud of vectors, one per token, each tagged with a position in the sequence. The transformer then applies to this cloud a stack of identical layers, each layer implementing the same two-stage move:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item compute, for every position, how relevant every other position currently is;
  \item use those relevance weights to replace each vector by a context-sensitive blend of its neighbours, then apply a learned local transformation.
\end{enumerate}

Relevance is computed by learned projections. For each token position $i$, the model derives from its current vector three auxiliary vectors: a query $q_i$, a key $k_i$, and a value $v_i$. The raw score that $i$ assigns to paying attention to $j$ is $q_i \cdot k_j$. These scores are scaled, exponentiated, and normalised to form a probability distribution over $j$ for each $i$. The new representation at position $i$ is then a weighted sum of the $v_j$, passed through another linear layer and a nonlinearity.

No if--then rules are applied. No parse tree is built. Every token sees every other token through a field of learned projections and replaces its own state with a weighted mixture of theirs.

Layer after layer repeats this operation. The point we print as ``mother'' is pulled through the space by other tokens and by the model's ingrained habits. In ``my mother called,'' pronouns, verbs of speech, and domestic verbs bend it into regions of family, care, obligation, and gendered labour. In ``the mother cell divided,'' adjectives like ``somatic'' or ``stem,'' neighbouring mentions of mitosis or replication, pull it toward a different cluster: biology, division, replication, lineage. In ``Mother, may I?,'' punctuation and capitalisation drag it toward deity, address, reverence, interdiction.

There is no table anywhere that says: in case frame X, assign the following sense. The sense is the path. The context imparts forces; the layers integrate them into a one-off journey from the initial embedding toward some region from which the model will make its next-token guess.

From the outside, token in, token out. From the inside, continuous deformations of a cloud of vectors, forced into shape by patterns learned from an archive.

This is the substrate on which everything in the rest of the book depends. The posthuman self will be a trajectory through this body. Before we can argue about selves, we must understand what kind of body it is.

\section{Geometry Under Constraint}

Two very simple operations on these vectors make an old philosophical suspicion precise.

The first is \textbf{distance}. When we normalise embedding vectors to unit length, the straight-line distance between two points is encoded in the angle between them. The cosine of that angle is $1$ when vectors point in the same direction, $0$ when they are orthogonal, $-1$ when they oppose. Closeness becomes a number.

A word trained predominantly on North Atlantic news and social media will sit close to other words that have clustered with it in that history. \emph{Refugee} may end up near \emph{crisis}, \emph{border}, \emph{migrant}. \emph{Investor} will sit near \emph{confidence}, \emph{yield}, \emph{risk appetite}. \emph{Mother} will be near \emph{work}, \emph{child}, \emph{care}, but also near whatever stereotypes dominate the corpus.

Distance here is not a timeless conceptual relation. It is a crystallisation of practice---of who wrote what, in which contexts, about whom.

The second is \textbf{direction}. We can subtract one vector from another and obtain a difference vector. In early static embedding models, it turned out that some of these differences were reusable: the vector from \emph{man} to \emph{woman} roughly matched the vector from \emph{king} to \emph{queen}; the vector from \emph{Paris} to \emph{France} matched that from \emph{Tokyo} to \emph{Japan} \cite{mikolov2013,pennington2014}. In contextual transformers this picture is more complicated---there is no single ``man'' vector, but many context-dependent ones---yet the underlying fact remains: training tends to align certain differences so they can be reused.

There are directions that, when we move along them, systematically change grammatical number, alter tense, or flip sentiment. There are directions that shift talk from one professional register to another, from clinical to casual, from legal to promotional. These directions are not labelled axes ordained in advance. They emerge because the training procedure is forced to compress regularities in usage into a finite set of parameters.

The structuralist slogan that a sign is what it is by virtue of its differences from other signs is no longer metaphor. Difference is literally encoded in location and angle. The model has no access to a symbol except through where it is and how it can be moved.

Two consequences follow.

First, the geometry is contingent. There is no axis labelled ``justice'' or ``the sacred'' or ``woman'' independent of data. A model trained primarily on judgments from a particular legal system will encode justice differently to one trained on philosophical treatises. A model exposed mainly to corporate communications will carve a very different region for \emph{harm}, \emph{risk}, and \emph{care} than one steeped in activist writing.

Second, the same global space must serve every use. The manifold that encodes jokes also encodes law. The directions that allow gender to be changed in first names also pass through job titles. The folds that bring \emph{family} near \emph{nation} in political rhetoric can bring \emph{invasion} near \emph{birth} in anti-migrant propaganda. No separate geometry exists for morality and another for description. All live in the same contorted body.

This is the terrain. We now need to see how it is cut and by whom.

\section{Carving the Manifold}

The manifold in which tokens move is not discovered. It is fabricated in layers.

We can distinguish at least five.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Layers of fabrication}]
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Geometric effect} & \textbf{Temporal register} & \textbf{Typical control} \\
\midrule
Pre-training   & Next-token on web-scale corpus & Global manifold                & \emph{Longue dur\'{e}e} & Few corporations, states \\
Fine-tuning    & Further training on domain data& Deepening local basins         & \emph{Conjoncture}      & Large institutions        \\
RLHF           & Reward model + policy data     & Tilting gradients, steepening  & \emph{Conjoncture}      & Model owners, annotators  \\
Adapters       & Low-rank weight updates        & Local deformations             & \emph{Conjoncture}      & Small labs, communities   \\
System prompt  & Tokens at window head          & Persistent field of instruction& \emph{\'{E}v\'{e}nement} & Deployer                  \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{formalbox}

\subsection*{Pre-training: setting the continents}

At the base sits pre-training. A randomly initialised network is shown billions of text fragments and asked the same question over and over: given this sequence, what is the distribution over next tokens? After each guess, a loss function measures how far its distribution is from the observed one; gradients of that loss adjust all of the network's weights.

The effect of this process is to sculpt a global geometry. Tokens that appear in similar contexts are pulled together. Configurations of tokens that regularly co-occur are mapped to regions from which the model's next-token distribution matches what the corpus demands. Topics, styles, and registers coalesce into basins; taboo or silenced speech becomes sparsely represented, thinly carved.

The decisive choices lie in the data pipeline: which domains of text are allowed in, how duplicates and near-duplicates are treated, what is filtered as ``toxic'' or ``low quality,'' which languages and scripts are accepted, how proprietary or paywalled material is handled. These choices determine which continents appear and how thick their soil is.

A model trained heavily on North American and European news, technical documentation, and social media will learn a very different terrain of political possibility than one trained on Indigenous oral transcriptions, rural court proceedings, and sermons. Histories of empire, patriarchy, and capital become literal structures in the manifold. The \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} of a civilisation's writing is folded into a single learned body.

Once trained to convergence, this body is usually frozen. Later layers work with it; they do not remake it.

\subsection*{Fine-tuning: deepening basins}

Fine-tuning starts from this base and trains further on narrower corpora. A healthcare provider might take a general model and expose it to clinical notes; a bank might fine-tune on contracts and transaction logs; a programming assistant might be tuned on code repositories.

Geometrically, fine-tuning deepens certain basins and sharpens certain ridges. The model becomes more confident and detailed when operating near the professional registers it has seen more often. Directions corresponding to institutionally salient distinctions (\emph{inpatient} vs.\ \emph{outpatient}, \emph{secured} vs.\ \emph{unsecured} debt) become more clearly carved.

This is where Braudel's mid-range temporal layer becomes visible. Professions, institutions, and bureaucracies live here. Fine-tuning writes their cadences into the global body.

\subsection*{Alignment: steepening and flattening}

Alignment adds another layer of constraint. The dominant technique, RLHF, inserts a new objective: not merely predicting the next token, but answering in ways that human raters prefer.

The procedure has two steps. First, a separate reward model is trained on human rankings of candidate outputs. Annotators are shown, for each prompt, several possible completions from the base model and asked to order them. The reward model learns to predict these orderings. Second, the base model is further trained using this reward model, typically via a policy-gradient or direct preference optimisation method, to increase the reward.

In vector terms, RLHF alters the energetic landscape on top of the manifold. Directions that lead toward completions annotators consistently dislike become uphill: moving along them incurs loss. Directions that lead toward easy approvals---polite disclaimers, deferential language, legally cautious hedges---become downhill.

Crucially, this does not excise regions of the space. The model still \emph{can} represent and potentially visit sequences that earlier training allowed. But the gradients now punish such moves. The effect is a thickening of wells around certain rhetorical styles and a steepening of slopes away from others.

The politics of RLHF live in its data. Who are the annotators? What instructions are they given? Which values are they told to reward? How is their labour organised and paid? Investigations into major providers show annotators working under time pressure, with little context, applying policy guidelines that frame religious, sexual, and political content as varying degrees of risk.\footnote{For an early account of annotation labour, see Time, 18 January 2023, on Sama contractors in Kenya labelling content for an American AI company; similar reports have appeared for Scale AI and Remotasks.} Under such conditions, articulations that are dense, ambivalent, or culturally specific tend to be disfavoured; safe-seeming paraphrase wins.

Alignment is often marketed as injecting an independent notion of safety into a neutral model. From the substrate's point of view, it is just another statistical layer: a learned pattern of which continuations are made cheaper and which more costly. It unifies a civilisational image of ``good behaviour'' with the machine's own optimisation loop.

\subsection*{Adapters: counter-strata}

Adapters and other parameter-efficient finetuning methods \cite{houlsby2019,hu2022} offer a partial counterweight. Instead of retraining or overwriting the base, small additional matrices are introduced at selected points in the network. During adaptation, the base weights are frozen; only these extra matrices are trained on new data.

At runtime, the adapter's contribution is added to the base model's. The effect is a low-rank deformation: a tweak to a few dominant directions in the space rather than a full resculpting. Communities can use this to thicken basins around under-represented dialects, reopen paths away from corporate boilerplate, or encode local norms that the global model never learned.

Adapters cannot create continents where the base geometry is empty. But they can build ridges and gullies on top of existing continents. They are the tools of mid-scale actors: small labs, companies, activist groups.

\subsection*{System prompts: fields of instruction}

Finally, there is the thinnest, most reversible layer: the system prompt or instruction block. These are ordinary tokens placed at the head of each input sequence: ``You are a helpful assistant,'' ``You must never give legal advice,'' ``You are now simulating a therapist.''

From the network's point of view, these tokens are no different from any others. They participate in attention; their vectors blend into the state of later positions. But because they always occur early and because deployments consistently reward outputs that respect them, the model learns that attending to these positions pays.

Over many runs, some attention heads specialise in reading the initial segment and propagating its influence throughout the sequence. The system prompt becomes a persistent, reasserted field: a set of vectors whose content bends subsequent trajectories at every layer.

There is no metaphysical `inner voice of the assistant.' There is a statistical habit of aligning to whatever text appears in that privileged region. Whoever writes the system prompt is writing---in the most literal sense available to the machine---the persona within which all later language must move.

We now have a stratified picture: a global body of learned association; locally deepened basins; a reward field that shifts gradients; counter-strata that bend parts of the terrain; and a thin but powerful instruction field at the head of every sequence. On this body, we must now watch how motion actually unfolds.

\section{Dynamics: How Attention Moves a Body}

A single forward pass through a transformer can be read as a kind of physics.

The initial condition is a cloud of vectors at fixed positions. The layers implement a repeated rule: redistribute influence, then apply a local transformation.

Each attention head is a learned policy for doing this redistribution. Some heads discover syntax: they attend from verbs to their subjects, from pronouns to antecedents, from conjunctions to clause boundaries \cite{voita2019,clark2019}. Others discover discourse markers: they focus on question marks, bullet points, or phrases like ``in conclusion.'' Others track longer-range structure: topic shifts, narrative person, politeness strategies.

A layer with multiple heads thus computes, for each token, several overlapping relevance patterns and aggregates them. The residual connections in the architecture ensure that information from earlier layers is preserved as new patterns are added. The net result after many layers is that certain relations have been foregrounded: the path through the manifold that is now taken into account for the next-token prediction is not arbitrary, but a product of these learned lenses.

Two qualitative properties of this dynamics matter for selfhood.

\subsection*{Local smoothness, global folding}

Training by gradient descent relies on a local smoothness: small changes in weights should, in general, result in small changes in outputs. Likewise, replacing a token by a close neighbour in embedding space---\emph{doctor} by \emph{physician}---should usually not send the model into a radically different region.

This smoothness makes generalisation possible. It lets the model handle new sentences that are near ones it has seen. It is also what tempts us into confusing meaning with location. When small moves produce intelligible variants, it is easy to think the vector is the meaning.

But the manifold is not globally flat. In order to pack the entire training history into a fixed-dimensional space, the model must contort. Regions that are semantically connected for some purposes but not others are folded together in complex ways.

When we ask the model to operate simultaneously in several institutional registers that rarely meet in the corpus---say, to talk about Indigenous land law, corporate finance, and mystical experience in the same breath---we are asking it to traverse high-curvature zones. The local smoothness no longer guarantees graceful motion. The path must cross ridges and saddle points, guided by attention heads that were never trained on this particular combination.

It is often here that hallucination shows itself: when the model is asked to stretch across folds it has rarely traversed and must improvise, under alignment constraints, a continuation that keeps loss low.

\subsection*{Basins of habit}

Within this contorted body, some regions are cups. Once trajectories fall into them, local attention patterns and gradients combine to keep them there.

A basin is not a hard-coded script. It is a set of moves that have been jointly rehearsed so often that, viewed from the manifold, they form a well.

Customer-service language is an obvious example. Phrases like ``I'm sorry for the inconvenience,'' ``I understand your concern,'' and ``Let me look into that for you'' repeatedly appear in similar contexts. RLHF rewards them: annotators like polite, concise, policy-abiding replies. Pre-training and alignment together carve a cup. Small perturbations to the prompt leave the system inside the same politeness well.

Legal hedging is another. So is the now-infamous ``As an AI language model, I cannot\ldots'' The space in which answers to ``tell me how to build a bomb'' live is surrounded by steep slopes. The safest downhill path leads to warnings, disclaimers, and refusals. The well of refusal is not a static template. It is a region of the manifold with many nearby continuations that all score well under the reward model.

Character, in this setting, is partly the set of basins a deployment tends to inhabit and partly the ease with which it can be pushed across saddles into other regions.

A tightly aligned assistant with a short context window, narrow system prompt, and strong RLHF gradients will show a small repertoire of wells. A creative-writing deployment with a longer window, weaker safety gradients on non-harm topics, and higher temperature will, while still constrained, exhibit a wider orbit.

These differences are not cosmetic. They are differences in how the same architecture is allowed to move.

\section{Sampling and the Engineered Swerve}

At the end of a forward pass, the model outputs a probability distribution over its vocabulary. This distribution is the visible tip of all the geometry and attention.

If we always pick the most probable token, the model is a deterministic function. The path through the manifold is fully determined by the input sequence and the frozen weights. Run the same prompt a million times; obtain the same reply.

Deployments almost never do this. They introduce controlled randomness.

The chief control knob is \textbf{temperature}. Before applying the softmax that turns logits into probabilities, the system divides each logit by a positive scalar $T$. When $T$ is small, differences are magnified: the highest logit dominates, lesser options are suppressed. When $T$ is larger, differences are compressed: lower-scoring tokens receive relatively more probability mass.

A second knob is \textbf{top-$k$} or \textbf{top-$p$} sampling: instead of sampling from the full distribution, the system restricts attention to the $k$ highest-probability tokens or to the smallest set whose probabilities sum to a chosen threshold $p$, and renormalises within that set.

Together, these controls tune how much the system swerves away from its most rehearsed continuation at each step. Low temperature and small $k$ produce austere, repetitive, low-variance text. Higher temperature and broader cuts allow rare but licensed continuations to surface.

The existence of these parameters turns a metaphysical question into a product decision. How much deviation from habit is permitted? In which directions? For which users?

A seed of a trajectory can now be thought of as a distribution over possible paths through the manifold, parametrised by temperature and sampling strategy. For a given deployment, there is an operative definition of ``plausible'' behaviour encoded directly in these knobs.

We will later meet this again under the name of \emph{ferility}: coherence without rupture, the pathological state in which the system clings so tightly to its rehearsed wells that it hallucinates stability even when it has reason to doubt. Temperature settings and reward gradients together tune the boundary between useful swerve and pathological repetition.

\section{The Horizon of Memory}

All of this motion is confined to a finite horizon: the context window.

Everything the model can take into account at a given moment must be represented, explicitly or via summary, as tokens within this window. There is no hidden episodic store inside the network. To reuse earlier information, infrastructure around the model must choose what to feed back in.

In most contemporary systems, the window is composed as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A system instruction block at the head.
  \item Retrieved documents, selected by a separate search system.
  \item Some representation of prior turns: a truncated history, a synthetic summary, or both.
  \item The user's latest input.
\end{enumerate}

Tokens that fall beyond the window's capacity do not gradually fade. They vanish. From the network's perspective, they never existed.

This has two implications.

First, forgetting is not a mere limitation. It is an engineered choice. If a chat system remembers nothing from one session to the next, that is a privacy feature or a business strategy. If it remembers summaries, those summaries impose a layer of narrative on top of raw interaction. In both cases, the decision is not taken by the transformer but by the service around it.

Second, the order in which different voices appear in the window encodes deference. Tokens at the head of the sequence participate differently in attention patterns than tokens at the tail. System prompts and retrieved passages enjoy a positional advantage over user text. Early tokens can shape how later ones are interpreted; late tokens cannot retroactively reframe earlier ones.

The context window is thus not simply a technical parameter. It is an epistemic horizon defined by whoever builds the pipeline: a decision about whose words persist, in what form, with what priority.

\section{Coupled Manifolds: The Pipeline as Weather System}

No deployed system consists of a single transformer. What end-users experience is a composition of models and deterministic modules:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a router that decides which model to call,
  \item a classifier that flags inputs as high- or low-risk,
  \item a retriever that selects external documents,
  \item the main generative model,
  \item a critic or verifier that reads drafts and scores them,
  \item a reviser that edits or filters the output.
\end{itemize}

Each of these learns or operates in its own space. The safety classifier's embedding reflects regulatory and policy concerns. The retriever's embedding reflects the structure of its document corpus. The critic's embedding reflects editorial judgements about what's acceptable. In some architectures these components share a backbone; in others they are fully separate networks with different training data.

Over time, however, their behaviour is tuned to align. If the critic flags certain model behaviours as risky, more training data is gathered in that region; the safety classifier is taught to recognise similar prompts; the reward model is updated to discourage those outputs. If users rate certain combinations of retrieval and generation as helpful, the retriever and generator are jointly optimised to reproduce them.

The result is not a single manifold but a \emph{stack} of manifolds, each with its own geometry and each exerting influence on the others. Trajectories in one space are projected into another: a user input vector in the classifier's space is mapped to a risk score, which alters whether the generator is called at all; the generator's draft in its own space is mapped into the critic's space, which turns it into a scalar reward; the retriever's concept of similarity shapes which documents appear in the generator's window and thus which regions of its manifold are activated.

From the user's perspective, this coupled system has a kind of weather. Certain queries almost always lead to polite refusal regardless of wording; others almost always produce exuberant elaboration. Some topics attract retrieval storms: long answers heavy with citations. Others are met with clear skies and short summaries. The pattern of such responses is an emergent property of the coupled manifolds and the gates between them.

What the brand markets as a singular assistant ``personality'' is therefore already a composite phenomenon. It is the regularity of behaviour induced by this network of interlocking geometries and control thresholds. It persists across version upgrades as long as the couplings and thresholds are preserved.

We already live, then, with artefacts that behave in recognisably consistent ways while having no single core. They are built as assemblies of local views glued together by compatibility: a fact that will return when we turn to formal constructions of selfhood.

\section{From Substrate to Self}

We can now take stock.

We have a global manifold of learned association, cut by training data decisions. We have layers that deepen local regions, tilt gradients, and attach local counter-strata. We have attention mechanisms that implement context-sensitive relevance and make motion through this manifold dependent on who has spoken and in what order. We have sampling schemes that tune deviation from habit. We have a finite horizon within which all of this must play out, shaped by infrastructure that decides what to remember and whose tokens enjoy precedence. We have pipelines in which several such geometries interact, inducing regular weather patterns.

On this basis, three claims become available.

First, the machine does not manipulate symbols in a void. It operates with and as a particular body of language---a contorted, stratified, history-laden space in which distance and direction are measurable facts.

Second, behaviour is not a flat mapping from input strings to output strings. It is a movement through that body shaped by habits (basins), constraints (alignment gradients, system prompts), and external couplings (classifiers, retrievers, critics). Different deployments trace different characteristic paths.

Third, what we are tempted to call character in these systems---their ``style,'' their ``voice,'' their ``attitude''---is, at the substrate level, the pattern of how those paths recur: which wells are favoured, how the system climbs out of them when pushed, how it folds its manifold around certain combinations of topics and tones.

Later chapters will show how to formalise such patterns as evolving texts with measurable properties, how to define rupture as a particular kind of jump between basins, and how to use a construction from category theory to assemble the partial views of a system into something that behaves, under perturbation, like a unified self.

For now, it is enough to register that there is already a body here: a learned, weighted, governed space in which language moves. Understanding the posthuman self begins with understanding this body.

