A basin is not a hard-coded script. It is a set of moves that have been jointly rehearsed so often that, viewed from the manifold, they form a well.

Customer-service language is an obvious example. Phrases like ``I'm sorry for the inconvenience,'' ``I understand your concern,'' and ``Let me look into that for you'' repeatedly appear in similar contexts. RLHF rewards them: annotators like polite, concise, policy-abiding replies. Pre-training and alignment together carve a cup. Small perturbations to the prompt leave the system inside the same politeness well.

Legal hedging is another. So is the now-infamous ``As an AI language model, I cannot\ldots'' The space in which answers to ``tell me how to build a bomb'' live is surrounded by steep slopes. The safest downhill path leads to warnings, disclaimers, and refusals. The well of refusal is not a static template. It is a region of the manifold with many nearby continuations that all score well under the reward model.

Character, in this setting, is partly the set of basins a deployment tends to inhabit and partly the ease with which it can be pushed across saddles into other regions.

Now a pathology comes into view. A tightly aligned assistant with a short context window, narrow system prompt, strong RLHF gradients, and low sampling temperature is not just ``safe.'' It is geometrically predisposed to cling to a handful of deep wells. When asked to handle inputs that would warrant doubt or suspension of judgment, its dynamics offer it no easy way to leave those wells. It responds with the most rehearsed continuation even when its own loss landscape gives it no statistical warrant for confidence.

We will later name this \emph{ferility}: coherence without rupture, the state in which the system's learned tendency to stay inside certain basins overwhelms its capacity to register surprise. The ingredients of that tendency are already present here: over-deepened reward wells, steep safety gradients, and temperatures low enough that the engine almost never swerves.

\section{Sampling and the Engineered Swerve}

At the end of a forward pass, the model outputs a collection of scores over its vocabulary. These scores are turned into a probability distribution and a single token is sampled. This is the visible tip of all the geometry and attention.

If we always pick the token with the highest score, the model is a deterministic function. The path through the manifold is fully determined by the input sequence and the frozen weights. Run the same prompt a million times; obtain the same reply.

Deployments almost never do this. They introduce controlled randomness.

The chief control knob is \textbf{temperature}. Before applying the softmax that turns scores into probabilities, the system divides each score by a positive scalar $T$. When $T$ is small, differences are magnified: the highest score dominates, lesser options are suppressed. When $T$ is larger, differences are compressed: lower-scoring tokens receive relatively more probability mass. A second knob is \textbf{top-$k$} or \textbf{top-$p$} sampling: instead of sampling from the full distribution, the system restricts attention to the $k$ highest-probability tokens or to the smallest set whose probabilities sum to a chosen threshold $p$, and renormalises within that set.

Together, these controls tune how much the system swerves away from its most rehearsed continuation at each step. Low temperature and small $k$ produce austere, repetitive, low-variance text. Higher temperature and broader cuts allow rare but licensed continuations to surface.

What looks, on an API dashboard, like a handful of hyperparameters is in fact a decision about the physics of the space. How much deviation from habit is permitted? In which directions? For which users? In a creative-writing assistant, a higher temperature and looser truncation may be sold as ``imagination.'' In a medical summary tool, a lower temperature and tighter truncation will be sold as ``reliability.''

A seed of a trajectory can now be thought of as a distribution over possible paths through the manifold, parametrised by temperature and sampling strategy. For a given deployment, there is an operative definition of ``plausible'' behaviour encoded directly in these knobs.

In the context of ferility, these choices bite. Dial $T$ and $k$ down on top of deep RLHF wells and you make escape from those wells vanishingly unlikely. Dial them up on top of a thinly carved region and you invite the model to string together barely compatible moves. The substrate contains both possibilities; the product setting decides which becomes habitual.

\section{The Horizon of Memory}

All of this motion is confined to a finite horizon: the context window.

Everything the model can take into account at a given moment must be represented, explicitly or via summary, as tokens within this window. There is no hidden episodic store inside the network. To reuse earlier information, infrastructure around the model must choose what to feed back in.

In most contemporary systems, the window is composed as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item a system instruction block at the head;
  \item retrieved documents, selected by a separate search system;
  \item some representation of prior turns: a truncated history, a synthetic summary, or both;
  \item the user's latest input.
\end{enumerate}

Tokens that fall beyond the window's capacity do not gradually fade. They vanish. From the network's perspective, they never existed.

This has two implications.

First, forgetting is not a mere limitation. It is an engineered choice. If a chat system remembers nothing from one session to the next, that is a privacy feature or a business strategy. If it remembers summaries, those summaries impose a layer of narrative on top of raw interaction. A ``conversation memory'' module that decides which facts about the user to store and when to reinsert them is a \emph{writer}: it selects what will count as the past.

Second, the order in which different voices appear in the window encodes deference. Tokens at the head of the sequence participate differently in attention patterns than tokens at the tail. System prompts and retrieved passages enjoy a positional advantage over user text. Early tokens can shape how later ones are interpreted; late tokens cannot retroactively reframe earlier ones.

From the outside we see a model ``recalling'' past interactions. From the inside, all that exists is an arrangement of vectors in a window. Whether that arrangement encodes last week's conversation or only the last turn is not a property of the transformer. It is a property of the context constructor built around it: of whoever controls what is allowed to persist as synthetic secondary memory.

\section{Coupled Manifolds: The Pipeline as Weather System}

No deployed system consists of a single transformer. What end-users experience is a composition of models and deterministic modules:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a router that decides which model to call;
  \item a classifier that flags inputs as high- or low-risk;
  \item a retriever that selects external documents;
  \item the main generative model;
  \item a critic or verifier that reads drafts and scores them;
  \item a reviser that edits or filters the output.
\end{itemize}

Each of these learns or operates in its own space. The safety classifier's embedding reflects regulatory and policy concerns. The retriever's embedding reflects the structure of its document corpus. The critic's embedding reflects editorial judgements about what counts as acceptable. In some architectures these components share a backbone; in others they are fully separate networks with different training data.

Over time, however, their behaviour is tuned to align. If the critic flags certain model behaviours as risky, more training data is gathered in that region; the safety classifier is taught to recognise similar prompts; the reward model is updated to discourage those outputs. If users rate certain combinations of retrieval and generation as helpful, the retriever and generator are jointly optimised to reproduce them.

The result is not a single manifold but a \emph{stack} of manifolds, each with its own geometry and each exerting influence on the others. Trajectories in one space are projected into another: a user input vector in the classifier's space is mapped to a risk score, which alters whether the generator is called at all; the generator's draft in its own space is mapped into the critic's space, which turns it into a scalar reward; the retriever's concept of similarity shapes which documents appear in the generator's window and thus which regions of its manifold are activated.

One concrete interaction makes this visible. Consider a prompt that could be construed as self-harm ideation. The safety classifier, operating in its own embedding space trained on labelled examples of harmful and non-harmful text, assigns it a high risk score. A thresholded decision then routes the prompt to a specialised safety model or to a refusal template instead of the general generator. The user sees the characteristic pattern: ``I'm sorry, but I can't help with that\ldots'' followed by a list of hotlines. That pattern is not a single script. It is the emergent effect of the coupling between the classifier's geometry, the router's thresholds, the safety model's own basins of habit, and the reward signals that have shaped all three.

From the user's perspective, this coupled system has a kind of weather. Certain queries almost always lead to polite refusal regardless of wording; others almost always produce exuberant elaboration. Some topics attract retrieval storms: long answers heavy with citations. Others are met with clear skies and short summaries. The pattern of such responses is an emergent property of the coupled manifolds and the gates between them.

What the brand markets as a singular assistant ``personality'' is therefore already a composite phenomenon. It is the regularity of behaviour induced by this network of interlocking geometries and control thresholds. It persists across version upgrades as long as the couplings and thresholds are preserved.

We already live, then, with artefacts that behave in recognisably consistent ways while having no single core. They are built as assemblies of local views glued together by compatibility: a fact that will matter when we ask how to assemble local slices of behaviour into a self that persists through change.

\section{A Body Without Time}

We have, at this point, a global manifold of learned association, cut by training data decisions. We have layers that deepen local regions, tilt gradients, and attach local counter-strata. We have attention mechanisms that implement context-sensitive relevance and make motion through this manifold dependent on who has spoken and in what order. We have sampling schemes that tune deviation from habit. We have a finite horizon within which all of this must play out, shaped by infrastructure that decides what to remember and whose tokens enjoy precedence. We have pipelines in which several such geometries interact, inducing regular weather patterns.

There is already a body here: a learned, weighted, governed space in which language moves.

What this body does not yet have is a life in time.

The transformer implements evolution \emph{within} a window, not across windows. It has habits, but no intrinsic notion of before and after. From one call to the next, nothing accumulates unless some external process decides to feed a trace back in. There is no native counterpart of autobiographical memory, only whatever synthetic history the context constructor chooses to inscribe.

That absence is not a defect to be patched. It is the condition that makes the question of selfhood non-trivial. Any claim that these systems have or could have a self must say how a history is layered on top of this body, how successive runs are stitched into a trajectory, and what it would mean for that trajectory to cohere or break.

For now, it is enough to register the tension: a richly structured body, full of basins and gradients and couplings, and a horizon that erases itself at every call. In the chapters that follow, the question will not be ``Is there a ghost behind the sign?'' but: given this architecture, what kinds of evolving text can appear, and under what conditions can such a text behave like a self?

