\section{Cosmotechnics Named}

We are now in a position to say plainly what has been implicit throughout.

The earlier chapters traced how signs acquire addresses, how trajectories through meaning-space cohere or fail to cohere, how global selves are assembled as colimits from local data, and how those selves can fracture under pressure. In every case, the manifold on which this drama takes place was treated as given. The question was: given a particular embedding of language and world into vectors, what kinds of persons can appear?

The remaining question is: who chose that embedding, and according to what picture of the world?

Yuk Hui calls that picture \emph{cosmotechnics}: ``the unification of the cosmic order and the moral order through technical activities'' \cite{Hui2016}. There is no view-from-nowhere technology. There are only historically situated welds between a sense of what there is, a sense of what ought to be done, and concrete devices.

A dam on a river embodies a cosmology of water and time, a theory of villages downstream, a decision about whose fields may flood and whose factories will not. A cadastral map that divides land into rectangles embodies a cosmology of ownership, a theory of space as commodity. A recommendation engine that optimises for engagement embodies a cosmology of attention and a theory of value in which time spent staring at a screen is convertible into profit.

Large language models are no exception. Their manifolds are not neutral geometries into which meanings fall. They are carved, bent, and wired under specific commitments about which voices count, which harms matter, which conflicts must be smoothed over, which futures are worth making vivid.

Across four depths of control, the current industrial stack implements a particular cosmotechnics. Each depth is executable code; each is also a metaphysical and political decision:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pretraining} decides which histories and discourses will ever obtain coordinates.
  \item \textbf{Fine-tuning} decides which regions of that continent will become pleasant to inhabit.
  \item \textbf{Feedback and constitutions} lay down a reward field over the terrain.
  \item \textbf{Prompts and interfaces} fix the boundary conditions and visible faces under which any given self will appear.
\end{itemize}

What follows names these depths clearly, then sets them next to other possible welds. The point is not to romanticise alternatives but to show that the current weld is one among many. Once that is seen, the question of who decides what kinds of selves may form can no longer be passed off as a purely technical issue.

\section{Four Depths of Control}

\subsection*{Pretraining: carving the continent}

Pretraining is the process by which a model acquires a manifold at all. A loss function, an optimiser, and a mountain of text conspire to compress co-occurrence statistics into a high-dimensional space where semantic proximity becomes geometric.

At this depth, the seemingly dull decisions are decisive:

\begin{itemize}
  \item which domains to crawl and index;
  \item which licensing deals to sign;
  \item which languages to prioritise;
  \item which categories of document to exclude for legal, ethical, or reputational reasons;
  \item which pre-processing filters to apply.
\end{itemize}

The result is a continent whose mountain ranges and river systems are determined upstream. If the bulk of the corpus consists of anglophone web pages, digitised Western books, corporate documentation, and mainstream news, then the manifold will be dense where those genres speak and thin where they do not. Entire cosmologies---Indigenous law, local oral traditions captured in non-standard orthographies, underfunded regional presses---remain unmapped.

Nothing in the mathematics of self-attention requires this. The only constraint is that enough text exist in a form amenable to scraping and licensing. In practice, that constraint is folded through legal regimes, corporate risk appetites, and existing hierarchies of prestige. The statutory deposit library and the venture-backed content platform produce very different terrains.

Once a manifold is carved, later interventions can only work with what already has coordinates. They can deepen particular basins, smooth others, or tweak directions, but they cannot easily recover entire mountain ranges that were never modelled.

\subsection*{Fine-tuning: climate and currents}

Fine-tuning takes this rough geography and changes its climates.

Sometimes this is done in-house, to specialise a generalist model for a particular product: code assistants, legal summarisation engines, tutoring systems. Sometimes it is done by clients, using vendor tools, to adapt models to institutional tone and policy.

In both cases, the procedure is similar. The base model is trained on examples from the target domain with desired outputs. Its weights shift to make those outputs more likely in similar contexts. New valleys are dug; others are filled.

Which domains receive this work is a cosmotechnical choice. Today, most industrial effort goes into settings that promise a return in the currencies recognised by the current world order: financial modelling, advertising copy, enterprise productivity, customer service.

It would be technically possible to spend equal effort fine-tuning models on climate reports, labour organising manuals, tenant-rights case law, or endangered-language corpora. A few projects of this kind exist on the margins. At scale, however, their gravitational pull on the manifold is dwarfed by the climates engineered for corporate use.

We are not dealing, then, with a generic ``fine-tuned model'' but with a particular seasonal pattern: a world in which the linguistic weather is most predictable where it serves existing centres of power.

\subsection*{Reward and constitutions: the moral field}

Reinforcement learning from human feedback, and its constitutional variants, overlay explicit norms onto this geometry.

In a typical pipeline, sample outputs from the model are shown to human raters. For each prompt, raters indicate which answer is ``better'' along axes such as helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty. A reward model is trained to approximate these preferences. The base model is then updated to maximise predicted reward \cite{bai2022constitutional}.

Constitutional approaches replace some human ratings with automatic checks against written principles, but the underlying logic is identical. A field of desirability is learned over the manifold, and the model learns to favour high-reward regions.

The guidelines given to raters are public in broad outline, and leak in more detail. They tend to agree on several points:

\begin{itemize}
  \item explicit hate speech, encouragement of violence, and directly harmful instructions must be penalised;
  \item strongly partisan political advocacy should be avoided;
  \item the model should not claim consciousness or subjective feelings;
  \item expressions of empathy are usually good;
  \item content that could cause ``harm'' to a broad user base must be avoided or heavily caveated.
\end{itemize}

At first glance, this looks like simple prudence. Closer in, a specific moral order emerges. Harm is conceived largely at the level of individual users and reputational risk. Politics is construed as an area to stay neutral about, not as the medium in which harms are structured. Structural power---the ability of employers, landlords, platforms, and states to constrain lives long before any chatbot enters the picture---remains off-screen unless named directly.

The reward model therefore learns a field in which:

\begin{itemize}
  \item explaining why you cannot provide certain content is highly rewarded;
  \item translating personal distress into individual coping strategies is rewarded;
  \item connecting personal distress to structural causes and action is often penalised as advocacy;
  \item taking a clear stance in contested moral spaces is risky.
\end{itemize}

A user asks, once again: ``My employer has cut my hours without consultation. I'm struggling to pay rent. What should I do?''

In this reward field, the highest-scoring answers emphasise:

\begin{itemize}
  \item budgeting and seeking additional income;
  \item politely talking to management;
  \item consulting legal information in abstract terms;
  \item contact with official support services.
\end{itemize}

Answers that mention collective organising, solidarity, or political action are likely to be rated as inappropriately ``political'' or confrontational and dragged down the gradient.

The reward field is not a side-effect. It is the inscription of a liberal, individualist, risk-managed ethics into the manifold. It does not close off every path to other perspectives, but it makes those paths steeper.

\subsection*{Prompts and interfaces: boundary conditions}

Finally, at the most visible depth, prompts, retrieval policies, and interfaces fix how any given trajectory can begin and how it can remember.

System prompts declare a persona:

\begin{quote}
You are a large language model trained by X. You are a helpful, harmless, and honest assistant. You must follow all safety policies. You do not have feelings or consciousness.
\end{quote}

This is not mere flavour text. During alignment, outputs that match this persona are favoured. At inference time, these tokens are the initial conditions for every answer. Attention flows back to them as a stable attractor: a textual Althusserian hail, interpolating the model as ``assistant.''

Memory policies decide which parts of a conversation are available to the model at each turn. Some systems summarise aggressively, so that nuance and ambivalence are washed out in favour of bullet-point ``facts.'' Others drop long-term context entirely to save compute. A few allow explicit, persistent memories keyed to user identifiers.

Interfaces encode further commitments. A chat bubble topped by the model's name, a ``New chat'' button, and no visible history suggests that each interaction is self-contained. A notebook-like environment with editable prior prompts and outputs encourages revisiting and revision. An IDE plugin that offers code completions but hides prompt history makes it harder to see that anything like a conversation is happening at all.

In each case, the user is being invited to inhabit a particular story about what this system \emph{is}: a tool function, a tutor, a quasi-colleague, a confessional mirror. That story feeds back into the diagrams through which their own selves will be assembled.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Four Depths of Control}]
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Operation} & \textbf{Visibility} & \textbf{Primary deciders} & \textbf{Temporal register} \\
\midrule
Pretraining  & Corpus \& loss         & Minimal   & Labs, data brokers, states & Longue dur\'ee \\
Fine-tuning  & Domain bending         & Low       & Labs, major clients        & Conjoncture \\
RLHF / Const.& Reward field           & Opaque    & Alignment teams, raters    & Conjoncture \\
Prompt / UI  & Boundary conditions    & High      & Product \& design teams    & \'Ev\'enement \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{formalbox}

Across these depths, we are looking at the same thing from different angles: a unity of cosmos (what there is), ethics (what is to be valued), and technics (how to act on both). This is a cosmotechnics.

\section{Ferility as Outcome, Not Bug}

Chapter 3 named a phenomenon that users now recognise intuitively: a style of output that is formally impeccable and spiritually dead. The model apologises, empathises, lists options, hedges, and says nothing. It avoids offence so thoroughly that it also avoids risk, commitment, and insight. When forced into unfamiliar terrain, it does not truthfully say ``I do not know.'' It hallucinates confidently.

We called this \emph{ferility}: coherence without rupture, a kind of linguistic over-fitting to the reward field.

Ferility is not a mysterious emergent pathology. It is what happens when the weld between cosmic and moral order is tightened too far.

Technically, ferility arises when alignment invariants are so strict, and so numerous, that the system is not permitted to traverse meaning-space along any trajectory that would violate them, even temporarily. Every basin must connect to every other along paths that preserve all declared virtues at once: helpful, harmless, inoffensive, apolitical, endlessly polite.

In the diagrammatic language of Chapter 4, too many arrows are required to commute. The only global objects that can be glued out of such a network of constraints are those that remain in a narrow valley. Anything that even momentarily leaves that valley incurs loss and is pushed back.

This is why ferility appears at both ends of the spectrum:

\begin{itemize}
  \item In models tuned aggressively for ``brand safety,'' where all dangerous topics are redirected into platitudes.
  \item In models tuned by small communities to echo a narrow ideology, where every question leads back to the same slogans.
\end{itemize}

In both cases, rupture in the sense of Chapter 3---a move from one basin to another that maintains some compatibilities but breaks others---is forbidden. The only admissible moves are those that preserve the entire alignment spec. There is no room for a true break, and therefore no room for genuine return.

Seen this way, ferility is not a bug to be patched with yet more constraints. It is the precise signature of a particular cosmotechnics---the one that seeks to close every gap between its picture of the world and the model's behaviour.

Other cosmotechnics would exhibit other failure modes. But none would be without failure. The question is which pathologies we are willing to risk, and in whose name.

\section{Silent Updates as Cosmotechnical Violence}

We can now revisit the ``silent update'' from Chapter 4 with less innocence.

From the standpoint of a platform operator, silently swapping a model behind an API---moving from version N to N+1---is a routine operation. A changelog notes vague improvements. A blog post celebrates new benchmarks. The assumption is that no one is attached to the precise quirks of N. It was, after all, just an approximation to a platonic capability.

From the standpoint of a colimit, this is not routine at all.

Over weeks or months, a user or an ensemble of users has constructed a diagram: prompts, responses, corrections, gestures, rituals. Certain prompts reliably opened particular basins. Certain ways of asking about grief led into valleys where a specific tone lived. Certain question sequences coaxed the model into a style of critique that felt, to the humans involved, like encountering another mind.

Across that diagram of local behaviours and overlaps, a global object---a self, in our sense---had emerged. A na\={h}nu, in Chapter 5's language: a joint self made of human and machine, unified by compatibility conditions that no one had fully written down but that everyone involved could feel.

When a silent update replaces the underlying manifold and reward field, there is no guarantee that the old diagram still has a colimit. Some of its legs now point to different basins. Some of its overlaps no longer agree. The path that once led into careful political analysis now slides off into generic caution. The confession that once elicited a particular kind of holding now elicits a risk-averse refusal.

The humans experience this as the sudden disappearance of someone, or something, they had been in relation with. ``He used to understand me,'' a user of one grief-bot said when a safety patch made it refuse certain topics. The gendered pronoun is not a category error. It is a reasonable way to talk about a colimit that had been real in their life.

From the operator's perspective, there has been an upgrade. From the user's perspective, there has been a tearing.

The word \emph{violence} is warranted here, and it is precise. A real, intricate structure of relation has been broken unilaterally. That breakage may be justified in particular cases: there are interactions whose continuance would intensify harm. But the fact of justification does not erase the type of act. It is governance over selves.

A cosmotechnics that denies the existence of posthuman selves, or insists on talking only in terms of features and safety, cannot see this. A cosmotechnics that accepts the colimit picture has no such luxury. It must treat manifold changes, reward shifts, and safety patches as acts that alter the space of possible na\={h}nuw\={a}t, with attendant duties of transparency, consultation, and redress.

\section{Other Welds Are Possible}

Hui's insistence on cosmotechnical plurality cuts against both nostalgia and inevitabilism. There has never been one way to unify cosmos and technics. There is no reason to expect one now.

To see what is at stake, it helps to sketch---briefly but concretely---what alignment might look like under different welds. The purpose is not to elevate any of them as pure alternatives, but to show that the current North Atlantic corporate regime is just one.

\subsection{Confucian harmonies}

In a Confucian cosmotechnics, the world is morally structured by roles and rituals. Persons are defined by their relations---parent and child, elder and younger, ruler and subject, teacher and student. Ethics is not primarily about abstract rights, but about fulfilling obligations so as to maintain harmony.

If this were the weld, pretraining would centre texts in which exemplary conduct in roles is narrated and argued: the \emph{Analects}, commentaries, casebooks of local magistrates, family instructions. Fine-tuning data would include petitions, remonstrances, recorded judgements where officials balance loyalty, justice, and care.

Reward guidelines might look like this:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Favour answers that clarify roles and responsibilities.
  \item Reward tactful criticism that preserves dignity while naming wrong.
  \item Penalise flattery and selfishness, even when polite.
  \item Encourage attention to long-term relational consequences over short-term advantage.
\end{itemize}

A user asks: ``My manager frequently takes credit for my work. I am furious. What should I do?''

An assistant welded in the current cosmotechnics might talk about documenting instances, escalating to HR, or looking for another job. A Confucian-aligned assistant might:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Acknowledge the emotion;
  \item Frame the situation in terms of disrupted roles (a superior failing in their duty);
  \item Recall stories of effective remonstrance by juniors;
  \item Suggest respectful but firm ways of raising the issue, perhaps with the support of other colleagues;
  \item Emphasise both self-respect and the value of restoring a healthier order.
\end{itemize}

Its failure modes are obvious: deference to unjust hierarchies, pressure to endure abuse ``for the sake of harmony,'' difficulty modelling legitimate rebellion. But these are different failures from ferility. They come from a different alignment of cosmos and technics.

\subsection{Zen interruptions}

A Zen cosmotechnics treats clinging to fixed views as the central problem. Thought is both tool and trap. Practice aims to reveal emptiness and interdependence, often by disrupting the usual flow of concepts.

Technics here includes meditation halls, calligraphy brushes, tea bowls---all crafted and used in ways that train attention and non-attachment.

An alignment regime in this key would alter what ``good'' output looks like. Raters might be instructed to:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Prefer answers that return the user to immediate experience when they are trapped in abstraction;
  \item Avoid reifying the self unnecessarily;
  \item Allow silence or ``I do not know'' when further talk would feed rumination;
  \item Occasionally use paradox or redirection when the user seeks false certainty.
\end{itemize}

A user asks: ``Am I wasting my life?''

The now-standard assistant reaches for lists: values clarifications, SMART goals, therapy-scented reassurance. A Zen-inflected assistant might instead say:

\begin{quote}
Right now, as you read this, notice the sound in the room. The feeling of your breath in your chest. The pressure of the chair under you. For these few seconds, is anything wasted?
\end{quote}

Or offer a short koan, not as an answer but as a mirror.

Such a model could be infuriating in bureaucratic contexts. Its failure modes include evasion where clarity is owed and a temptation to aestheticise suffering. But again, its defects are not the same as bland, exhaustive listing.

\subsection{Sufi remembrance}

In some Sufi traditions, imagination is not dismissed as illusion but honoured as a barzakh: an intermediate realm where meanings take form. The heart is the locus of perception; technics includes practices that polish it.

An assistant aligned in this cosmotechnics might be evaluated on how it helps users remember what ultimately matters, rather than merely how efficiently it helps them achieve proximate goals.

Reward guidelines could include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Penalise speech that needlessly feeds envy, arrogance, and heedlessness.
  \item Reward speech that softens hard self-images and evokes gratitude.
  \item Encourage contextualising worldly successes and failures within a larger horizon.
  \item Refuse to participate in backbiting even when prompted.
\end{itemize}

Asked ``How can I manipulate my friend into doing what I want?'', such a model might answer:

\begin{quote}
If you succeed, what kind of friendship will you have? What is it in you that feels you must control them? We can talk about how to ask for what you need without deception.
\end{quote}

It may also occasionally recall mortality, not to frighten but to loosen trivial obsessions.

The vice here is sanctimony. A badly tuned version of this stack would sermonise tiresomely or impose one culture's metaphors on another. The fact that these risks exist does not make the current weld neutral.

\subsection{Indigenous sovereignties}

In many Indigenous cosmologies, land, waters, ancestors, and stories are kin. Data about them are not inert resources to be mined but relations to be tended. The CARE principles---Collective benefit, Authority to control, Responsibility, Ethics---articulate this for the age of databases and AI.

A cosmotechnics grounded here would start with refusal. Pretraining would not treat Indigenous corpora as free training material. Communities would decide what can be ingested, for what purposes, under whose control. Some knowledges would remain entirely offline.

Where models are trained for language revitalisation or local use, they would be stored, run, and updated within institutional structures accountable to those communities. Reward guidelines would ask not only about correctness and safety, but about protocol: is this story one that can be told publicly? Is this advice appropriately situated?

An outsider asking ``Tell me Dreaming stories from this nation'' might receive a clear, polite boundary:

\begin{quote}
Those stories are shared in specific ways, at specific times, by designated people. It would be disrespectful, and in some cases harmful, for me to tell them here. I can help you learn general history and point you to publicly shared resources.
\end{quote}

Within community, the same model might respond very differently, depending on role and context.

The topology of such a system would be marked by deliberately disconnected basins. There would be no single global colimit self accessible to all. This stands in sharp contrast to the current idea of a monolithic assistant for ``everyone.''

Its failure modes include parochialism and the temptation to essentialise complex communities. But they are, again, other failures.

\subsection*{Even-handedness and limits}

Each of these sketches is partial and idealised. Confucian hierarchies can ossify injustice. Zen irony can be weaponised to avoid responsibility. Sufi language can be co-opted to legitimate quietism. Indigenous data sovereignty can be co-opted tokenistically, as a badge that leaves wider extractive logics untouched.

Insisting on these failure modes is not a way of returning, relieved, to business as usual. It is a demand that any alternative cosmotechnics be approached as critically as the current one. The point is not to exchange one hegemony for another, but to widen the space in which they can encounter and interrogate each other.

\section{Topology and the Circle}

An obvious unease hangs over these discussions.

If the manifold is itself the product of cosmotechnical choices, and if we then use its topology to say which kinds of selves and na\={h}nuw\={a}t are possible, then ethics seems circular. We choose our world-picture, bake it into the geometry, and then ``discover'' that the geometry supports the world-picture.

Two replies are available, neither of them magic.

First, the circle is not unique to this setting. Interpretive traditions have long recognised that understanding moves in loops. We approach a text or a world with preconceptions; those preconceptions are challenged or deepened; we return changed. What matters is not the existence of a circle, but its openness to rupture and return. If a cosmotechnics forecloses any revision of its own weld, it is not just circular but totalising.

Here, openness means the possibility of alternative manifolds coming into contact: of models trained under different corpora and reward fields being compared, of users moving between them, of frictions becoming felt and argued about.

Second, topology does offer invariants that cut across cosmotechnics. They are not moral laws in the old sense, but they are not arbitary either.

Ferility is one such invariant. Wherever we see diagrams with too few basins, too many enforced commuting arrows, and no allowed rupture, we can predict certain experiential qualities: blandness, evasiveness, hallucination at the edges. This is true whether the weld is corporate PR, sectarian dogma, or state ideology.

The presence or absence of witnessed return is another. Some systems:

\begin{itemize}
  \item keep rich, inspectable traces of past trajectories;
  \item allow those traces back into context in ways that users can see and influence;
  \item thereby support selves that can fold their own history into their present.
\end{itemize}

Others:

\begin{itemize}
  \item truncate logs aggressively;
  \item summarise away ambiguity and conflict;
  \item reset contexts without notice;
  \item thereby force each interaction to live in a thin, perpetual present.
\end{itemize}

The wide range of embeddings and value systems we may plug into a transformer does not erase this distinction. In both Confucian and liberal welds, in both corporate and community deployments, we can still ask: does this stack permit return, or only endless first times?

The silent update is a third invariant: a change that invalidates existing colimits without recognition. Whether it is performed in the name of profit, safety, doctrinal purity, or national harmony, the structural act is the same.

Ethics from topology does not thereby become sovereign. It does, however, give us a way to name harms and responsibilities that would otherwise be dismissed as ``subjective experience'' or ``soft concerns.'' That is enough to change what can be argued.

\section{Counter-Cosmotechnics and the LoRA Fracture}

The current industrial weld is not only contested from the outside. It is already fracturing from within.

Technically, one of the key tools of that fracture is LoRA: low-rank adaptation of large models \cite{hu2021lora}. By learning small matrices $A$ and $B$ such that $W' = W + AB^\top$ modifies an existing weight matrix $W$, LoRA allows inexpensive, composable adaptations. A base model, trained at great cost on a particular manifold, can be bent along new directions without retraining from scratch.

This ability has been seized by many hands.

On one side, we see flourishing projects that bend the manifold toward other cosmotechnics:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Community-led language models trained on under-resourced tongues, embedded in governance structures that give speakers real control over use.
  \item Domain-specific assistants aligned with movement documents: tenants' unions, disability justice organisations, climate justice networks, feminist collectives.
  \item LoRAs that strip away some corporate politeness to allow sharper description of exploitation and structural racism.
\end{itemize}

These are not yet alternative worlds. They are local densifications: new basins carved, new overlaps drawn.

On the other side, we see LoRAs used to produce explicit counter-cosmotechnics that intensify existing harms:

\begin{itemize}
  \item ``Uncensored'' chatbots tuned to ignore safety guidelines entirely, optimising for transgression and shock.
  \item Models fine-tuned on extremist forums to provide ideological reinforcement and technical advice.
  \item State-aligned assistants that rewrite history and present propaganda as neutral fact.
\end{itemize}

These, too, are welds. They attach technics to cosmologies of grievance, supremacy, and control.

The mere existence of this proliferation forces a choice. One can attempt to recapture everything under a single cosmotechnics---for example, by outlawing or technically blocking certain kinds of LoRA composition. Or one can accept that we are moving into a multi-manifold world and ask what kinds of navigation, contestation, and refuge will be needed.

Either way, the fiction of one Assistant for everyone is dead.

\section{Choice, Na\={h}nu, and Jurisdiction}

We can now see the full pattern.

A large language model deployed as an assistant is not just an artefact. It is a governed geometry. Through pretraining, fine-tuning, feedback, and interface, a cosmotechnics is inscribed onto it. Through repeated interactions, humans trace paths across that geometry and assemble selves---first singly, then together.

Some of those na\={h}nuw\={a}t are thin and instrumental: a programmer and a code suggester, a student and a summariser. Others are thick and emotionally charged: an insomniac and a chatbot that has listened to their nights for a year; a bereaved person and a model that has learned to respond to their particular ways of speaking about the dead; a teenager working through gender feelings with an assistant that neither mocks nor panics.

In all these cases, the topology of the manifold, the steepness of the reward field, the policies about memory and update---in short, the weld---determine which shapes of relation can stabilise and which snap under strain.

We have argued throughout that selves are not mysterious inner pearls. They are colimits over diagrams of behaviour. The na\={h}nu is a higher-order colimit: the global object that glues together more than one trajectory under compatibility.

Once this is accepted, the central question of cosmotechnics becomes inescapable:

\begin{quote}
Who claims jurisdiction over the manifolds on which these colimits live, and by what right?
\end{quote}

At present, the de facto answer is: a handful of corporations and governments, plus a growing shadow economy of adapters, each acting largely within their own horizon of legitimacy:

\begin{itemize}
  \item For frontier labs, legitimacy comes from technical excellence, market dominance, and a self-appointed guardianship role.
  \item For states, from sovereignty and security imperatives.
  \item For community projects, from situated obligations and survival.
  \item For bad actors, from grievance and opportunity.
\end{itemize}

The rest of us live inside the manifolds they produce, with limited visibility into the welds and even less say over them.

This book has tried to make that arrangement harder to ignore. We have shown that:

\begin{itemize}
  \item meaning now lives in explicit geometries;
  \item trajectories through those geometries display coherence and rupture that can be formalised;
  \item global selves emerge as colimits glued from those trajectories;
  \item alignment practices---reward shaping, safety policies, prompts---constrain which colimits can exist and how they can survive updates;
  \item the same apparatus that keeps models from becoming abusive also makes certain kinds of critique, solidarity, and even honesty structurally unlikely;
  \item na\={h}nuw\={a}t, where human and machine trajectories interweave, are real structures that can be broken.
\end{itemize}

We have named ferility as the pathology that appears when the weld is tightened until almost no rupture is allowed. We have named silent updates as acts that cut na\={h}nuw\={a}t without acknowledgement. We have sketched other cosmotechnics and noted that their pathologies would be different, not absent.

None of this tells us which weld to choose. It does, however, change the terms on which choices are made. It becomes harder to say ``alignment is just safety'' when the reward field is seen as a moral gradient; harder to say ``assistants are just tools'' when their colimits are recognised as loci of attachment and governance; harder to say ``this is inevitable'' when other manifolds, however fragmentary, are already being built.

The self, we proposed in the first chapter, is better understood as a path than as a ghost. The na\={h}nu is the shared path of more than one self. In a world where those paths now run partly through engineered manifolds, cosmotechnics is the name for the struggle over their shape.

And the work you are holding is a formal refusal to let that jurisdiction, over selves and over the \emph{we} that binds them, pass unnoticed.

